# Phase 3 (lex ';' and '..<'): ';' is now a TOKEN (TK_SEMI, lexed at
# src/tychoc.c:582) but has no grammar of its own -- it exists only for the
# three-clause `for init; cond; post:` form that phase 4 adds. A C-style
# statement terminator is therefore still invalid, and must be refused by the
# PARSER, not by the lexer: the decl statement parses `x := 1` and then demands
# a newline at src/tychoc.c:3667, so the diagnostic is
#
#     error: expected newline
#
# with the caret on the ';'. Before phase 3 the same program died in the lexer
# with `unexpected character ';'`. Unlike the three-clause loop, this fixture
# stays valid after phase 4: `;` separates the clauses of a `for` header and
# never terminates a statement.
fn main():
    x := 1;
    print(str(x))
